// logos.std.wql.writ_graph — the NATIVE materialization behind the `deem!`
// graph-source parameter (`g: &Writ`, ADR 0016 M1a): the generic edge
// relation of a Writ object graph, as one flat Vec of 8-tuples.
//
// The STATIC twin of the runtime engine's virtual scan (bind_source_tree,
// std.deem exec): identical vocabulary and identical graph discipline —
//   • one row per EDGE, preorder from the document root;
//   • the VIRTUAL ROOT EDGE (parent = 0) is UNCONDITIONAL — every document is
//     a row, INCLUDING a null-rooted (empty) one, whose whole relation is that
//     one row. The relation's CARDINALITY is therefore binding-time
//     independent: this walker and the dynamic ts_scan answer the same count
//     for the same document. An `if root.is_null() { return }` here made an
//     empty document have no graph statically and a one-row graph
//     dynamically — one vocabulary cannot have two cardinalities;
//   • every edge emits (a shared child appears once per incoming edge);
//   • a container's children EXPAND exactly once (visited set on handles) —
//     DAGs don't duplicate subtrees, cycles terminate;
//   • container child ids are the Writ node handles (stable, resolvable);
//     scalar-leaf ids are synthetic FNV(parent handle, edge ordinal) — a
//     leaf's VALUE word is not an identity (equal ints share a Pod word,
//     equal strings may intern). The virtual root edge is a row of the SAME
//     relation and takes the SAME rule at its own coordinates: parent word 0
//     (the reserved non-handle), ordinal 0. So a non-container root — null,
//     i64, bool, f64, str alike — has child id FNV(0, 0), and that is not a
//     free choice for either binding time;
//   • TOM rows (schema'd tiny object maps) expand with idx = the 6-bit field
//     key code; other ref tags surface as kind "obj" (open world — visible
//     and joinable, expanded once the per-tag traverse protocol lands).
//
// Row tuple, in column order (the deem! walker registers these names):
//   (parent, key, idx, child, kind, tag, vi, vs)
//    i64     str  i64  i64    str   i64  i64 str
// `vi` carries an i64 leaf value (bool as 0/1), `vs` a string leaf value —
// concrete types (the static engine has no dyn tier); `kind`/`tag`
// discriminate. `str` cells are VIEWS into the source document: the Writ
// must outlive the returned Vec (the generated fn's borrow does this).
package logos.std.wql.writ_graph;

use logos.lang.writ.container;    // Writ
use logos.lang.writ.anyval;       // WAny — tag dispatch + type_code
use logos.lang.writ.array;        // WArray
use logos.lang.writ.wmap;         // WMap<WString, WAny> / WMap<Wu6, WAny>
use logos.lang.writ.wstring;      // as_wstr — null-safe str view
use logos.lang.str;
use logos.lang.num.float;   // f64_to_bits — f64 leaves ride vi as IEEE bits
use logos.lang.arith;             // wrapping_mul — FNV mixes wrap by design
use logos.mem.collections.vec;
use logos.lang.stream;             // BatchStream / RowsBatch / RowMajor / Rewind (ADR 0025)
use logos.lang.option;             // Option — the cursor's pull answers with one

// One graph edge row (the shared vocabulary; see the module doc).
pub type WritEdgeRow = (i64, str, i64, i64, str, i64, i64, str);

// ── THE VIRTUAL ROOT EDGE'S COORDINATES — one convention, four sites ────────
// A document's root has no incoming edge, so the relation gives it a VIRTUAL
// one whose coordinates are fixed by RULING and not read off the document.
// These constants ARE that ruling, and they are `pub` because the convention
// has four sites in three packages:
//
//   • writ_graph_edges, below                  — the STATIC Writ walker;
//   • logos.mem.deem's ts_scan                 — the DYNAMIC Writ walker;
//   • logos.mem.compiler.metaprog.derive_graph_source, INSIDE ITS QUOTE — the
//     #[derive_graph_source] materializer for native structs, which emits the
//     coordinates into the user's module;
//   • logos.std.wql.lower's gp_build           — THE READER. Every graph path
//     anchors on `__ga.parent == WG_ROOT_PARENT`, so a producer and the reader
//     disagreeing by one literal makes every `from g .field …` answer nothing.
//
// The row EMITTER was collapsed once already (the root row is a row of the same
// relation and fills its columns by the same wg_node_id / wg_kind / wg_vi as
// the walk), which is what stopped the value-axis drift — a lost f64 `vi` arm,
// then a `child` id spelled `1`. What an emitter cannot absorb is the
// COORDINATE: a producer passes it in and the reader compares against it. Three
// hand-written triples and a hand-written anchor were what remained, and this
// names them. A fifth site is now a `use`, not a fourth guess.
//
// WHY 0 IS AVAILABLE AS "no parent node": no real node id is 0 — a Writ
// container id is a live handle and a derived struct id is its address.
pub const WG_ROOT_PARENT: i64 = 0i64;
pub const WG_ROOT_KEY:    str = "";
pub const WG_ROOT_IDX:    i64 = 0i64 - 1i64;
// The root edge's ORDINAL among the (absent) parent's children — the second
// word the synthetic-leaf id rule hashes. Only the two producers that COMPUTE
// an id consume it; a derived struct's id is its address, not a fold.
pub const WG_ROOT_ORD:    i64 = 0i64;

// ── The source trait (ADR 0016 §6) ──────────────────────────────────────────
// THE canonical registration of Writ as a deem! source: a `g: &Writ` param
// carries this one-rel vocabulary, addressable as the param itself
// (`from g ** .engine e …`). This declaration IS the mechanism — the compiler
// keeps only a bootstrap-path fallback seed that defers to it.
pub trait GraphSource {
    rel edge(parent: i64, key: str, idx: i64, child: i64,
             kind: str, tag: i64, vi: i64, vs: str);
}
impl GraphSource for Writ {
    rel edge = writ_graph_edges;
}

fn wg_fnv2(a: i64, b: i64) -> i64 {
    let mut hh: u64 = 14695981039346656037u64;
    hh = wrapping_mul(hh ^ (a as u64), 1099511628211u64);
    hh = wrapping_mul(hh ^ (b as u64), 1099511628211u64);
    return hh as i64;
}

fn wg_kind(v: WAny) -> str {
    if v.is_map()     { return "map"; }
    if v.is_array()   { return "arr"; }
    if v.is_tinymap() { return "tom"; }
    if v.is_string()  { return "str"; }
    if v.is_null()    { return "null"; }
    if v.is_bool()    { return "bool"; }
    if v.is_float()   { return "f64"; }
    if v.is_int()     { return "i64"; }
    return "obj";
}

fn wg_is_container(v: WAny) -> bool {
    return v.is_map() || v.is_array() || v.is_tinymap();
}

fn wg_seen_has(seen: &Vec<u64>, h: u64) -> bool {
    let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
    while i < seen.len() {
        if seen.get(i) == h { return true; }
        i = i + 1i64;
    }
    return false;
}

// THE node-id rule, ONE spelling for every row of the relation: container ⇒
// the real Writ handle; anything else ⇒ the synthetic FNV(parent word, edge
// ordinal). `parent_word` is a WORD, not a WAny, because the VIRTUAL ROOT EDGE
// has no parent node — it passes the reserved 0 (see writ_graph_edges).
//
// It is a FUNCTION and not two copies on purpose. The root row and the walk
// rows are rows of one relation, and every time the rule has been written
// twice the copies have drifted: the root row lost its f64 `vi` arm once
// (fixed 9dcf2b3c) and its very EXISTENCE for a null root once. A second
// spelling is the defect, not the risk of one.
fn wg_node_id(parent_word: i64, child: WAny, ord: i64) -> i64 {
    if wg_is_container(child) { return child.raw() as i64; }
    return wg_fnv2(parent_word, ord);
}

// THE `vi` rule, likewise one spelling: the TOTAL typed i64 payload column,
// canonical filler 0 for everything that is not int/bool/float.
fn wg_vi(child: WAny) -> i64 {
    let mut vi: i64 = 0i64;
    if child.is_int()       { vi = child.as_i64(); }
    else if child.is_bool() { vi = if child.as_bool() { 1i64 } else { 0i64 }; }
    // f64 leaf: vi carries the IEEE-754 BIT PATTERN (kind == "f64" tells the
    // consumer to reinterpret via f64_from_bits) — vi is the VALUE, and it is
    // bit-exact, so a stored −0.0 reads back as −0.0. It must NOT be keyed
    // through `f64_data_key`: that would turn the stored −0.0 into +0.0.
    // Before this, an f64 leaf was VISIBLE but its value silently dropped.
    //
    // ⚠ KNOWN GAP, the same one `deem/exec.logos` ts_walk records — see the
    // comment there, which is the canonical statement of it. This column is
    // BOTH the value and the Eq/Hash key of the set vocabulary, so `distinct`
    // and join over a float column separate −0.0 from +0.0 and separate two
    // NaNs of different payload, where the semantics `f64_data_key` gives
    // `order by` (PostgreSQL's, `lang/num/float.logos`) says all three are one
    // value. `order by e.vi` is i64 order over those bits, so negative floats
    // come back reversed. Closing it needs the value and the key to stop being
    // the same column; that is an arc, not an edit here.
    //
    // ⚠ AND `f64_canon_bits` (2026-08-07) DOES NOT CLOSE IT, which is worth
    // saying because it closes the sibling gap in the incremental min/max map.
    // There the canonical bits are the KEY and the accumulator holds the VALUE,
    // so collapsing the key costs nothing. Here one column is both, so keying
    // it canonically would DESTROY the stored −0.0 and the stored NaN payload —
    // the very loss the paragraph above pins. The gap is a MISSING SECOND
    // COLUMN, not a missing key function. It is DETERMINISTIC —
    // it does not move with the optimiser — so it is a semantics gap, not a
    // correctness hazard.
    else if child.is_float() { vi = f64_to_bits(child.as_f64()) as i64; }
    return vi;
}

fn wg_emit(out: &mut Vec<WritEdgeRow>, parent: WAny, key: str, idx: i64,
           child: WAny, ord: i64) {
    out.push((parent.raw() as i64, key, idx,
              wg_node_id(parent.raw() as i64, child, ord), wg_kind(child),
              child.type_code() as i64, wg_vi(child), child.as_wstr()));
}

fn wg_descend(child: WAny, out: &mut Vec<WritEdgeRow>, seen: &mut Vec<u64>) {
    if !wg_is_container(child) { return; }
    let hw: u64 = child.raw() as u64;
    if wg_seen_has(seen, hw) { return; }   // expanded already (DAG/cycle)
    seen.push(hw);
    wg_walk(child, out, seen);
}

fn wg_walk(v: WAny, out: &mut Vec<WritEdgeRow>, seen: &mut Vec<u64>) {
    if v.is_map() {
        let m: &WMap<WString, WAny> = v.as_map();
        let mut ord: i64 = 0i64;
        let mut s: i64 = 0i64;
        while s < m.slots() {                 // raw slots; empty key as null
            let kv: WAny = m.key_at(s);
            if !kv.is_null() {
                let cv: WAny = m.val_at(s);
                wg_emit(out, v, kv.as_wstr(), 0i64 - 1i64, cv, ord);
                wg_descend(cv, out, seen);
                ord = ord + 1i64;
            }
            s = s + 1i64;
        }
        return;
    }
    if v.is_array() {
        let a: &WArray<WAny> = v.as_array();
        let mut i: i64 = 0i64;
        while i < a.len() {
            let cv: WAny = a.get(i);
            wg_emit(out, v, "", i, cv, i);
            wg_descend(cv, out, seen);
            i = i + 1i64;
        }
        return;
    }
    if v.is_tinymap() {
        // a TOM (schema'd row): fields edge with idx = the 6-bit key code
        let t: &WMap<Wu6, WAny> = unsafe { &*(v.resolve() as *const WMap<Wu6, WAny>) };
        let mut ord: i64 = 0i64;
        let mut k: i64 = 0i64;
        while k < 52i64 {
            if t.contains(k as u8) {
                let cv: WAny = t.get(k as u8);
                wg_emit(out, v, "", k, cv, ord);
                wg_descend(cv, out, seen);
                ord = ord + 1i64;
            }
            k = k + 1i64;
        }
    }
}

/// The generic edge relation of `root`'s object graph (see the module doc for
/// the row vocabulary and the graph discipline). Row `str` cells are views
/// into the document — keep `root` alive while the rows live.
pub fn writ_graph_edges(root: &Writ) -> Vec<WritEdgeRow> {
    let mut out: Vec<WritEdgeRow> = vec_new::<WritEdgeRow>();
    let rv: WAny = root.root();
    // The VIRTUAL ROOT EDGE (parent = 0 → the root node): makes the root
    // itself queryable (it has no real incoming edge) and gives graph paths
    // their anchor (`parent == 0`) without threading root handles through
    // params. 0 is reserved: no real node handle is 0.
    //
    // UNCONDITIONAL — a NULL root gets this row too. It is the root of the
    // document the same way any other value is, and the relation's cardinality
    // must not depend on the binding time: the dynamic twin (`deem/exec.logos`
    // ts_scan) has never had a null guard, so an early return here made one
    // vocabulary answer 0 rows statically and 1 row dynamically for the same
    // empty document. A null root is not a container, so wg_node_id gives it
    // the synthetic FNV(0, 0); wg_kind says "null", type_code() says 0,
    // wg_vi's cascade leaves the canonical 0, and as_wstr guards on is_string
    // and yields "". wg_walk over a null is a no-op, so the row is the whole
    // relation.
    //
    // The columns come from the SAME wg_node_id / wg_vi the walk uses — the
    // root edge is a row of the same relation, so it must fill the same
    // columns by the same rule. Open-coding them here is what let this row
    // drift twice (a missing f64 `vi` arm, then this early return). The
    // COORDINATES come from the WG_ROOT_* constants above, which the other two
    // producers and the graph-path reader consume as well.
    out.push((WG_ROOT_PARENT, WG_ROOT_KEY, WG_ROOT_IDX,
              wg_node_id(WG_ROOT_PARENT, rv, WG_ROOT_ORD), wg_kind(rv),
              rv.type_code() as i64, wg_vi(rv), rv.as_wstr()));
    let mut seen: Vec<u64> = vec_new::<u64>();
    seen.push(rv.raw() as u64);
    wg_walk(rv, &mut out, &mut seen);
    return out;
}

// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// WritWalk — THE DOCUMENT WALK AS A CURSOR (ADR 0025 R-D)
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// `writ_graph_edges` above is the walk as a VALUE: it runs to completion and
// hands back every row of the relation in one `Vec`. That is a materialization
// the query plane did not ask for — criterion 1 counts nodes the PLAN inserts,
// and this one is inserted by the PRODUCER, below the count, which is why all
// 41 writ rel grounds in the corpus read `MG_CONTAINER` (no node) while a whole
// document sits in memory behind each of them.
//
// `WritWalk` is the same walk as a STREAM: it hands out the rows a batch at a
// time and never holds more than one batch. Three things had to be reified for
// that, and each is a place where the recursive version was keeping state on
// the machine stack:
//
//   1. THE RECURSION → AN EXPLICIT FRAME STACK. `wg_walk`/`wg_descend` are
//      mutually recursive, and a recursion cannot be suspended between two
//      rows. `WgFrame` is one activation of `wg_walk` — the container, which
//      of the three container shapes it is, how far the scan of its slots has
//      got, and the edge ORDINAL (which is not the slot index for maps and
//      TOMs, where absent slots are skipped and must not consume an ordinal,
//      because the ordinal is hashed into every scalar child's identity).
//
//   2. THE SEEN SET → AN OWNED FIELD. It was a `&mut Vec<u64>` threaded through
//      the recursion; here the cursor owns it, because the cursor IS the walk
//      and the set's lifetime is the walk's. It stays the same LINEAR set with
//      the same membership rule (`wg_seen_has`) — deliberately unchanged, so
//      the cursor's row sequence is decidable against `writ_graph_edges`'s by
//      a row-for-row differential rather than by argument. Its O(n²) is
//      inherited, is the same O(n²) the Vec producer has, and is OWED, not
//      fixed here: swapping in a hash set changes the answer's TIMING and not
//      the answer, so it belongs to a step that can measure it alone.
//
//   3. THE BATCH BOUNDARY → INVENTED. A document walk has no natural batch:
//      unlike a Memoria leaf (which is a page, a physical unit) or a `Buffer`
//      (which is one packet by definition), nothing in a Writ says where one
//      batch stops. So the boundary is a RULING — `WG_BATCH_CAP` rows, flushed
//      — and it is named here rather than passed in because a per-call batch
//      size would be a knob no caller has a reason to set and every caller
//      could set differently, and the emitted scan is identical for every
//      value of it. It is not a correctness parameter: the fixture
//      `wql_writ_walk_cursor` pins the SAME ROWS IN THE SAME ORDER for
//      capacities that split the corpus documents differently.
//
// THE RE-READ STORY IS A SECOND WALK, NOT A SECOND COPY, and that is the whole
// reason this type exists rather than a `Buffer` over `writ_graph_edges` — the
// route S6-A measured and REVERTED (+29 materialization nodes, +35,073 corpus
// bytes, 3 fixtures stopped compiling). A `Buffer` is a POSITIONED stream with
// ONE cursor: a rel block that reads the relation twice, or a self-join, drains
// a full copy per re-read. A walk has no position to share — `writ_walk(root)`
// called twice is two independent walks over one borrowed document, costing one
// frame stack each. `Rewind` is therefore implementable here HONESTLY (restart
// the walk: clear the stack, clear the seen set, re-arm the root row) and it is
// implemented below, but it is NOT the capability the plan should read for the
// re-read decision — see the note on the impl.
//
// FORWARD-ONLY. No `Bidirectional`, no `RandomAccess`, no `SizedStream`: a walk
// cannot retreat without re-walking, cannot seek to row n without walking n
// rows, and cannot answer its own row count without walking to the end. Each of
// those is a capability that must be ABSENT rather than emulated — an emulated
// `size()` here would walk the document to answer a question the planner asks
// for free elsewhere, which is the "capability with no consumer" the stream
// vocabulary refuses on principle.

/// The invented batch boundary (see the block comment). Rows, not bytes.
pub const WG_BATCH_CAP: i64 = 64i64;

// The three container shapes, as a frame tag. Spelled as constants rather than
// re-asked per step (`v.is_map()` …) because the frame's shape is decided ONCE,
// when the frame is pushed, and re-deriving it every step would be a second
// place where "which container is this" is answered.
const WGF_MAP: i32 = 0i32;
const WGF_ARR: i32 = 1i32;
const WGF_TOM: i32 = 2i32;

/// One suspended activation of `wg_walk`: which container, how far, which
/// ordinal comes next.
struct WgFrame {
    /// The container being expanded.
    node: WAny,
    /// `WGF_MAP` / `WGF_ARR` / `WGF_TOM` — decided at push time.
    kind: i32,
    /// The next SLOT to examine (map slot, array index, TOM key code).
    pos: i64,
    /// The next EDGE ORDINAL. Equals `pos` for arrays; for maps and TOMs it
    /// lags, because absent slots are skipped without consuming an ordinal.
    ord: i64,
}

/// The Writ document walk, as a forward-only batch stream of `WritEdgeRow`.
///
/// Yields exactly the rows `writ_graph_edges` returns, in exactly that order —
/// the virtual root edge first, then preorder over the graph, a shared child
/// once per incoming edge, containers expanded once. `str` cells are VIEWS into
/// the document, which is why the borrow is carried.
#[borrow_carrying]
pub struct WritWalk {
    /// The document, borrowed for the life of the cursor.
    doc: &Writ,
    /// The suspended recursion (item 1 above).
    stack: Vec<WgFrame>,
    /// The expanded-container set (item 2 above), owned.
    seen: Vec<u64>,
    /// The batch being filled. Handed out as a `RowsBatch<WritEdgeRow>` and
    /// cleared at the head of the next pull — which is why the emitted scan
    /// binds the batch inside its own block (`rexpr_walk::batch_scan_frag`):
    /// the next `next_batch()` takes `&mut self` and the batch's borrow must
    /// have ended.
    buf: Vec<WritEdgeRow>,
    /// False until the virtual root edge has been emitted and the root frame
    /// armed. `rewind` clears it.
    armed: bool,
}

impl WritWalk {
    /// A fresh walk over `root`. Costs one empty frame stack — it does NOT
    /// touch the document until the first pull.
    pub fn new(root: &Writ) -> WritWalk {
        return WritWalk {
            doc: root,
            stack: vec_new::<WgFrame>(),
            seen: vec_new::<u64>(),
            buf: vec_new::<WritEdgeRow>(),
            armed: false,
        };
    }

    /// The virtual root edge + the root frame — `writ_graph_edges`'s prologue,
    /// moved to the first pull so that constructing a cursor is free.
    ///
    /// ⚠ IT PUSHES THE ROOT ROW UNCONDITIONALLY, including for a null root, for
    /// the reason the module doc gives at length: the relation's cardinality is
    /// binding-time independent, and an empty document's whole relation is this
    /// one row. The columns come from the SAME `wg_node_id` / `wg_kind` /
    /// `wg_vi` the walk uses — a third spelling of the root row is exactly the
    /// drift the constants and the shared rules exist to stop.
    fn arm(&mut self) {
        let rv: WAny = self.doc.root();
        self.buf.push((WG_ROOT_PARENT, WG_ROOT_KEY, WG_ROOT_IDX,
                       wg_node_id(WG_ROOT_PARENT, rv, WG_ROOT_ORD), wg_kind(rv),
                       rv.type_code() as i64, wg_vi(rv), rv.as_wstr()));
        self.seen.push(rv.raw() as u64);
        if wg_is_container(rv) {
            self.stack.push(WgFrame { node: rv, kind: wg_frame_kind(rv),
                                      pos: 0i64, ord: 0i64 });
        }
        self.armed = true;
    }

    /// ONE STEP of the suspended recursion: advance the top frame to its next
    /// present child, emit that edge, and — if the child is an unexpanded
    /// container — push ITS frame, so the subtree comes before the sibling.
    /// That last line is what makes this preorder and not level order, and it
    /// is the explicit-stack image of `wg_emit` immediately followed by
    /// `wg_descend`.
    ///
    /// Returns false when the stack is empty, i.e. the walk is over.
    fn step(&mut self) -> bool {
        while self.stack.len() > 0i64 {
            let ti: i64 = self.stack.len() - 1i64;
            let fk: i32 = self.stack.get(ti).kind;
            let fv: WAny = self.stack.get(ti).node;
            let mut p: i64 = self.stack.get(ti).pos;
            let od: i64 = self.stack.get(ti).ord;
            if fk == WGF_MAP {
                let m: &WMap<WString, WAny> = fv.as_map();
                // Skip absent slots WITHOUT consuming an ordinal — the same
                // `if !kv.is_null()` the recursive walk has, with the loop
                // turned inside out.
                while p < m.slots() && m.key_at(p).is_null() { p = p + 1i64; }
                if p >= m.slots() { self.stack.pop(); }
                else {
                    let cv: WAny = m.val_at(p);
                    wg_emit(&mut self.buf, fv, m.key_at(p).as_wstr(), 0i64 - 1i64, cv, od);
                    self.stack.set(ti, WgFrame { node: fv, kind: fk,
                                                 pos: p + 1i64, ord: od + 1i64 });
                    self.push_child(cv);
                    return true;
                }
            } else if fk == WGF_ARR {
                let a: &WArray<WAny> = fv.as_array();
                if p >= a.len() { self.stack.pop(); }
                else {
                    let cv: WAny = a.get(p);
                    // The array's ordinal IS its index — both columns take `p`,
                    // exactly as `wg_walk`'s array arm passes `i` twice.
                    wg_emit(&mut self.buf, fv, "", p, cv, p);
                    self.stack.set(ti, WgFrame { node: fv, kind: fk,
                                                 pos: p + 1i64, ord: p + 1i64 });
                    self.push_child(cv);
                    return true;
                }
            } else {
                let t: &WMap<Wu6, WAny> = unsafe { &*(fv.resolve() as *const WMap<Wu6, WAny>) };
                while p < 52i64 && !t.contains(p as u8) { p = p + 1i64; }
                if p >= 52i64 { self.stack.pop(); }
                else {
                    let cv: WAny = t.get(p as u8);
                    // idx is the 6-bit FIELD KEY CODE and the ordinal is the
                    // dense count of present fields — they differ, and the
                    // recursive arm's two variables (`k` and `ord`) are these
                    // two frame fields.
                    wg_emit(&mut self.buf, fv, "", p, cv, od);
                    self.stack.set(ti, WgFrame { node: fv, kind: fk,
                                                 pos: p + 1i64, ord: od + 1i64 });
                    self.push_child(cv);
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }
        return false;
    }

    /// `wg_descend`, as a stack push: expand a container child exactly once.
    fn push_child(&mut self, child: WAny) {
        if !wg_is_container(child) { return; }
        let hw: u64 = child.raw() as u64;
        if wg_seen_has(&self.seen, hw) { return; }
        self.seen.push(hw);
        self.stack.push(WgFrame { node: child, kind: wg_frame_kind(child),
                                  pos: 0i64, ord: 0i64 });
    }

    /// Pulls the next batch — THE INHERENT DOOR the generated scan calls
    /// (`next_batch()`, not `BatchStream::next`; see `Buffer::next_batch` for
    /// the standing reason).
    ///
    /// AN EMPTY BATCH IS NEVER YIELDED HERE, and that is a statement about this
    /// stream and not a departure from §1's rule: the walk always has the
    /// virtual root row to give, so the first pull is non-empty for every
    /// document including the empty one, and every later pull either fills or
    /// the walk is over. `Some(empty)` remains legal in the vocabulary — this
    /// producer simply never needs it.
    pub fn next_batch(&mut self) -> Option<RowsBatch<WritEdgeRow>> {
        self.buf.clear();
        if !self.armed { self.arm(); }
        while self.buf.len() < WG_BATCH_CAP {
            if !self.step() { break; }
        }
        if self.buf.len() == 0i64 { return Option::None; }
        return Option::Some(self.buf.as_slice());
    }
}

// The walk IS a batch stream, and the batch is row-major (`WritEdgeRow` tuples
// in a slice), which is what makes the emitter spell the row `bb[j]`.
impl BatchStream<RowsBatch<WritEdgeRow>> for WritWalk {
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<RowsBatch<WritEdgeRow>> { return self.next_batch(); }
}
impl RowMajor for WritWalk {}

// ⚠ `Rewind` IS IMPLEMENTED AND IS NOT THE RE-READ CAPABILITY.
//
// Restarting a walk is honest and cheap — drop the frames, drop the seen set,
// re-arm — so refusing to implement `Rewind` would be a lie about this type.
// But `Rewind` is the wrong flag for the PLAN's re-read decision, and the
// distinction is the one that would otherwise become a permissive defect the
// corpus cannot see:
//
//   `rewind` re-lands the ONE stream the prelude bound. A rel block that reads
//   the relation inside an outer loop over the SAME relation needs TWO LIVE
//   POSITIONS, and rewinding the shared binding mid-outer-loop destroys the
//   outer one. Every other `Rewind` implementor (`SliceStream`, `Epoch`,
//   `Buffer`) is a positioned view over LANDED storage, where a second cursor
//   is a second view of rows that stay put; a walk has no landed rows to view
//   twice.
//
// What the re-read decision actually needs is "a FRESH CALL to this producer
// yields an independent walk", which is a property of the PRODUCER FUNCTION,
// not of the stream type. That capability DOES NOT EXIST YET — see the REFUSAL
// block below, which says what it would cost and what was measured without it.
impl Rewind for WritWalk {
    fn rewind(&mut self) {
        self.stack.clear();
        self.seen.clear();
        self.buf.clear();
        self.armed = false;
    }
}

/// The frame tag for a container value. Asked ONCE per frame, at push time.
fn wg_frame_kind(v: WAny) -> i32 {
    if v.is_map()   { return WGF_MAP; }
    if v.is_array() { return WGF_ARR; }
    return WGF_TOM;
}

/// The cursor form of `writ_graph_edges` — the producer a `rel` declares over
/// when it wants the walk STREAMED instead of landed.
pub fn writ_walk(root: &Writ) -> WritWalk {
    return WritWalk::new(root);
}

// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// REFUSAL — `rel edge` STILL DECLARES OVER `writ_graph_edges`, NOT `writ_walk`
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// The cursor exists, is differentiated row-for-row against the Vec producer
// (`pass/wql_writ_walk_cursor`), and is NOT what the `impl GraphSource for
// Writ` above declares over. That is a refusal with a measured ground, in the
// S6-A style, and this block is the measurement so the next round starts from
// a number rather than from the intention.
//
// WHAT WAS MEASURED (2026-08-15, one build, `rel edge = writ_walk` and nothing
// else changed; six writ-carrying corpus fixtures, `--gen-dir` byte counts, and
// `tests/logos/criterion1_materialization_instrument.sh`):
//
//   fixture                            gen bytes  baseline →   cursor
//   wql_writ_graph_e2e                              55,548 →   58,466
//   wql_gpath_e2e                                  161,641 →  168,622
//   query_tree_source_graph_e2e                    175,621 →  179,276
//   derive_graph_source_root_row                    41,719 →   42,013
//   deem_three_domain_join                          94,008 →   94,656
//   wql_graph_root_id_cross_document                43,609 →   46,161
//                                                  TOTAL   →  +17,048
//   criterion 1, N1 (materialization NODES)          2,703 →    2,706  (+3, same 188-fixture population)
//   criterion 1, D2 (emitted collection bindings)    3,954 →    3,933  (−21)
//   criterion 1, T  (historical text match)            622 →      601  (−21)
//   criterion 1, accounted                          83.38% →   83.83%
//
// ⚠ The N1 row FIRST READ "2,701 → 2,706 (+5)" — a POPULATION MIX caught by
// the round's audit: 2,701 is the pre-round 186-fixture baseline, 2,706 a
// 188-fixture measurement, so the subtraction crossed the two new fixtures.
// Cross-check: D1 5417→5421 = +4 plan lines, and the six writ fixtures' own
// plan-line delta is exactly +4 — the corpus delta is writ-local. And the
// three rows below N1 were MISSING: a refusal that lists only the costs is
// not the measurement it claims to be — the cursor route REDUCES emitted
// collection bindings and IMPROVES the accounted share while adding nodes.
//
// So the cursor route is BETTER THAN THE S6-A BUFFER ROUTE — that one was +29
// nodes, +35,073 bytes, and THREE FIXTURES STOPPED COMPILING; here all six
// still compile and answer — and it is still A REGRESSION on the node/byte
// axes, which is the thing this round refused to land. Criterion (a) is "at
// or below baseline", and +3 nodes / +17,048 bytes is above it.
//
// WHY THE REGRESSION IS THERE, exactly. Today every writ rel in the corpus
// grounds `MG_CONTAINER` — the producer returns a container, so
// `access_plan::ap_drain` refuses a node (`ap_offers` is false) and the whole
// document sits in a `Vec` the plan never counted. A cursor makes `ap_offers`
// TRUE, which drops every writ rel into `plan_walker::plan_prove_once`'s drain
// cascade — and its FIRST arm is a blanket: `if prog.has_rels()` drains EVERY
// rel of the query, on the ground `MG_REL_BLOCK`. Three of the six fixtures
// have rel blocks. The plan is right to drain: a rel block may read the source
// twice, and a walk cursor read twice is EXHAUSTED, not restarted.
//
// WHAT WOULD CLOSE IT, and why it is not one line. The plan must be able to
// say "no copy — READ IT AGAIN", which needs BOTH halves:
//
//   1. THE DECISION HALF (small, and its site is known): a capability meaning
//      "a fresh call to this producer yields an INDEPENDENT walk" — a marker on
//      the stream type, one `producer_impls_trait_` line in
//      `SemaChecker::native_source_spec` (`src/compiler/sema_expr.cpp`), one
//      letter through `plan_walker::pw_flag`, one `rel_*` field in
//      `params.logos`, a second refusal in `access_plan::ap_drain` beside the
//      `ap_offers` one, and a ground in `access_plan_decide_mode` beside
//      `MG_CONTAINER` / `MG_ORDERED_SOURCE`. It must NOT be `Rewind`: every
//      `Rewind` implementor (`SliceStream`, `Epoch`, `Buffer`) is a positioned
//      view over LANDED storage, and re-landing the ONE binding the prelude
//      made destroys the outer read that a rel block is in the middle of. That
//      confusion would be a permissive defect a green corpus cannot see.
//
//   2. THE EMITTER HALF, WHICH IS THE ONE THAT STOPPED THIS ROUND: today every
//      second read uses the PRELUDE BINDING BY NAME (`prm.rel_srcs[…]`, nine
//      sites in `rexpr_walk.logos`). A re-walk needs a FRESH `<mat>(<marg>)`
//      local bound at the second read site instead. The data is all there —
//      `rel_mat`/`rel_marg`/`rel_marg2`/`rel_itty` are on `MacroParams`, which
//      `rexpr_walk` already takes, and `plan_walker` already emits that call
//      text at three sites — but it is nine emitter sites, and the failure mode
//      of landing half of them is SILENT WRONG ANSWERS: a rel block reading an
//      exhausted cursor gets zero rows on its second pass and no diagnostic.
//      A refusal that keeps the answers is worth more than a half-landing that
//      keeps the byte count.
//
// ALSO OWED, and deliberately NOT done here, because moving a producer that no
// `rel` declares over is churn that cannot be measured: the DYNAMIC twin
// (`logos.mem.deem.graphsrc`'s `ts_walk`/`ts_descend`/`dyn_graph_edges`) and the
// DERIVED producer (`logos.mem.compiler.metaprog.derive_graph_source`, which
// emits `__gs_walk_<T>`/`__gs_edges_<T>` INSIDE ITS QUOTE — a FOURTH producer of
// this same row type, not three). The vocabulary stays single only if all four
// move in the step that moves the `rel`.
